# mail-light.list -- SMTP mail between machines on a LAN, in 600 lines.
#
# THE MAIL TRANSPORT.  It does exactly what "send mail between networked C900s"
# asks and nothing else: smtpd answers on port 25 and delivers into
# /usr/spool/mail, smtpsend hands a message to another machine's port 25.  A
# machine has one holder of port 25, and this is it.
#
# It needs the stack, so it `require's lists/net.list -- a dist that includes
# this and not that fails the build rather than shipping a daemon with no
# /dev/tcp to open.
# PACKAGE (os/dist/PACKAGE-FORMAT).  A list with a `package' line is an
# addressable unit of a release channel; one without is not, and that is how
# testing.list stays out of one.
package  mail-light
role     optional
# KINDS -- the packages this component publishes besides its runtime half.
# `bin' is the list below and is not written down; `src' is the complete
# corresponding source for every executable it installs, which
# os/dist/PACKAGE-FORMAT argues and which refuses without the producer's source
# map.  No `man': neither smtpd nor smtpsend has a page, so both ship
# undocumented, which os/dist/coverage.tab records.
kinds    src

require	lists/net.list

# smtpd receives.  It is /etc/smtpd rather than /bin/smtpd because it is a
# daemon nobody types, and it is an INETD SERVICE, not a standing daemon:
# /etc/inetd.conf carries an enabled `smtp' line, and inetd offers no service
# whose program is not installed -- so INSTALLING THIS LIST IS WHAT TURNS THE
# SERVICE ON, the same conf file opens no SMTP port without it, and nothing has
# to edit inetd.conf or be kept in step with it.  Nothing of the daemon is
# resident between callers; the switchboard's listener holds one of the stack's
# fifteen connections.
#
# A site that wants it standalone -- more than one delivery at a time, or no
# switchboard -- runs `/etc/smtpd -m N &' from rc.net and comments the smtp line
# out of inetd.conf.  Each pre-forked child is a channel held for as long as it
# waits, whether or not anybody calls (FINDINGS C-2).
#
# It runs as root -- it must, to append to another user's mailbox and to chown
# it afterwards -- and it accepts mail for any domain naming a local user.
# There is no relaying and no authentication, which is right for a private LAN
# and wrong for a machine that can be reached from outside it.
f	/etc/smtpd	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/smtpd

# smtpsend sends.  mail(1) invokes it on a pipe for a recipient containing an
# `@' (cmd-3.x/mail/send.c ssend()), exactly where it invokes uux for one
# containing a `!'; it is also usable by hand, reading the message on standard
# input.  Resolution is gethostbyname(), so /etc/hosts answers on a LAN and no
# nameserver is required -- there is no MX lookup at all, which is why this
# list is not blocked on the resolver ever having run.
#
# There is no queue anywhere in this transport.  A host that does not answer is
# a failure reported to the sender, and mail(1) then saves the letter in
# $HOME/dead.letter as it does for any other delivery failure.  Nothing is
# retried, so a machine that is off when mail is sent to it does not get that
# mail when it comes back.
f	/bin/smtpsend	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/smtpsend
